1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,589
This is MATH 239, video 10b. We solve exam-type problems on compositions. Video 10a gives the method. For each problem, pause the video and try it first.

2
00:00:14,039 --> 00:00:20,077
First, Tutorial 3, question 2, parts a to d. They restrict the lengths and the parts.

3
00:00:20,527 --> 00:00:27,844
Then Assignment 3, question 2: 100 odd parts, and parts that alternate between odd and even.

4
00:00:28,294 --> 00:00:33,243
Then Assignment 3, question 3: compare two sets with series algebra.

5
00:00:33,693 --> 00:00:39,133
Last, question 3 of the practice midterm: lengths and parts modulo 5.

6
00:00:39,583 --> 00:00:50,634
The practice midterm says: define a relevant set and a weight function, and justify how you get the generating series. So a full answer has four parts.

7
00:00:51,084 --> 00:00:58,892
First, the set. Write it as a union of Cartesian products of part sets, and say why the union is disjoint.

8
00:00:59,342 --> 00:01:05,294
Second, the weight function. For compositions, it is usually the size, the sum of the parts.

9
00:01:05,744 --> 00:01:09,029
Third, the lemmas. Name each lemma when you use it.

10
00:01:09,479 --> 00:01:14,962
The Product Lemma needs the weight of a list to be the sum of the weights of its entries.

11
00:01:15,412 --> 00:01:19,209
The String Lemma needs a set with no element of weight 0.

12
00:01:19,659 --> 00:01:28,150
Fourth, the answer. Write it as the coefficient of x to the n in a ratio of two polynomials, as the question asks.

13
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:42,488
Tutorial 3, question 2 a. A is the set of compositions whose length is congruent to 1 modulo 3. Find phi of A as a ratio of two polynomials. Pause the video and try it.

14
00:01:42,938 --> 00:01:47,802
The allowed lengths are 1, 4, 7, 10, and so on. They are purple.

15
00:01:48,252 --> 00:02:03,697
Any positive integer can be a part, so P is the set of positive integers. The compositions of length 3 j plus 1 form P to the 3 j plus 1. A is the union of these sets over all j.

16
00:02:04,147 --> 00:02:09,161
The union is disjoint, because each composition has exactly one length.

17
00:02:09,611 --> 00:02:21,195
The weight is the size. By the Sum Lemma, add the series of these sets. By the Product Lemma, each one is x over 1 minus x, to the power 3 j plus 1.

18
00:02:21,645 --> 00:02:30,114
Take out one factor x over 1 minus x. The rest is a geometric series in x over 1 minus x, cubed.

19
00:02:30,564 --> 00:02:43,620
Multiply the top and the bottom by 1 minus x, cubed. The top becomes x times 1 minus x, squared. The bottom becomes 1 minus x, cubed, minus x cubed.

20
00:02:44,070 --> 00:02:54,139
Expand both. The answer is x minus 2 x squared plus x cubed, over 1 minus 3 x plus 3 x squared minus 2 x cubed.

21
00:02:54,589 --> 00:03:03,272
Check with n equal to 4. The composition 4 has length 1, and 1 1 1 1 has length 4. So the count is 2.

22
00:03:03,722 --> 00:03:20,895
Now expand the series. Multiply both sides by the bottom, and compare the coefficients. The first four are 0, 1, 1, 1. After them, a n is 3 a n minus 1, minus 3 a n minus 2, plus 2 a n minus 3.

23
00:03:21,345 --> 00:03:29,516
At x to the 4: 3 times 1, minus 3 times 1, plus 2 times 1, is 2. This matches the list.

24
00:03:29,966 --> 00:03:38,371
Question 2 b. B is the set of compositions of even length with each part at most 3. Pause the video and try it.

25
00:03:38,821 --> 00:03:43,237
The allowed lengths are 0, 2, 4, and so on. They are purple.

26
00:03:43,687 --> 00:03:50,514
Step 1. The parts are 1, 2 and 3. Their series is x plus x squared plus x cubed.

27
00:03:50,964 --> 00:04:07,327
Step 2. The compositions of length 2 j form P to the 2 j. B is their disjoint union over j. By the Sum and Product Lemmas, phi of B is the sum of x plus x squared plus x cubed, to the 2 j.

28
00:04:07,777 --> 00:04:18,358
This is a geometric series in the square of phi of P. So phi of B is 1 over 1 minus the square of x plus x squared plus x cubed.

29
00:04:18,808 --> 00:04:27,213
To get one fraction of polynomials, expand the square on a grid. Each cell is the product of one term from each factor.

30
00:04:27,663 --> 00:04:41,850
Cells on one diagonal have the same power. The x to the 4 diagonal has 3 cells. So the square is x squared plus 2 x cubed plus 3 x to the 4 plus 2 x to the 5 plus x to the 6.

31
00:04:42,300 --> 00:04:52,732
So phi of B is 1 over 1 minus x squared minus 2 x cubed minus 3 x to the 4 minus 2 x to the 5 minus x to the 6.

32
00:04:53,182 --> 00:05:05,683
Question 2 c. C is the set of compositions with at least 100 parts. Pause the video and try it. The length has no upper bound. So we do not add one case for each length.

33
00:05:06,133 --> 00:05:09,739
Instead, cut each composition after part 100.

34
00:05:10,189 --> 00:05:15,586
The first piece has exactly 100 parts. It is in P to the 100.

35
00:05:16,036 --> 00:05:20,153
The second piece has zero or more parts. It is in P star.

36
00:05:20,603 --> 00:05:30,331
The two pieces give back the composition, and each composition gives exactly one pair. So C is P to the 100, times P star.

37
00:05:30,781 --> 00:05:38,760
The weight is the size. The size of the composition is the size of the first piece plus the size of the second piece.

38
00:05:39,210 --> 00:05:51,114
So the Product Lemma and the String Lemma apply. Phi of C is x over 1 minus x, to the 100, times 1 over 1 minus x over 1 minus x.

39
00:05:51,564 --> 00:06:00,332
Video 10a showed that 1 over 1 minus x over 1 minus x is 1 minus x, over 1 minus 2 x.

40
00:06:00,782 --> 00:06:09,955
So phi of C is x to the 100 times 1 minus x, over 1 minus x to the 100, times 1 minus 2 x.

41
00:06:10,405 --> 00:06:18,512
Question 2 d. D is the set of compositions of odd length with each part at least 2. Pause the video and try it.

42
00:06:18,962 --> 00:06:23,378
The allowed lengths are 1, 3, 5, and so on. They are purple.

43
00:06:23,828 --> 00:06:38,335
The parts are 2, 3, 4, and so on, with the series x squared over 1 minus x. The compositions of length 2 j plus 1 form P to the 2 j plus 1. D is their disjoint union.

44
00:06:38,785 --> 00:06:52,289
By the Sum and Product Lemmas, phi of D is the sum of phi of P to the 2 j plus 1. Take out one factor phi of P. The rest is a geometric series in phi of P squared.

45
00:06:52,739 --> 00:06:58,691
Substitute phi of P. Its square is x to the 4 over 1 minus x, squared.

46
00:06:59,141 --> 00:07:11,386
Multiply the top and the bottom by 1 minus x, squared. The result is x squared times 1 minus x, over 1 minus x, squared, minus x to the 4.

47
00:07:11,836 --> 00:07:20,327
Expand. The answer is x squared minus x cubed, over 1 minus 2 x plus x squared minus x to the 4.

48
00:07:20,777 --> 00:07:33,406
Check with n equal to 6. Here are the 5 compositions of 6 with parts at least 2. 2 4, 3 3 and 4 2 have even length, so they are not in D. The count is 2.

49
00:07:33,856 --> 00:07:51,093
Expand the series. Multiply both sides by the bottom, and compare the coefficients. The first four are 0, 0, 1, 1. After them, d n is 2 d n minus 1, minus d n minus 2, plus d n minus 4.

50
00:07:51,543 --> 00:07:57,922
At x to the 6: 2 times 1, minus 1, plus 1, is 2. This matches the list.

51
00:07:58,372 --> 00:08:10,212
Assignment 3, question 2 a. Count the compositions of n with exactly 100 parts, all odd. Give the series and a formula. Pause the video and try it.

52
00:08:10,662 --> 00:08:22,779
Let O be the odd positive integers. Its series is x plus x cubed, and so on, which is x over 1 minus x squared. The set is O to the 100.

53
00:08:23,229 --> 00:08:32,573
The weight is the sum of the parts. By the Product Lemma, the series is x to the 100 over 1 minus x squared, to the 100.

54
00:08:33,023 --> 00:08:41,258
Now find the coefficient. Divide out x to the 100. We need the coefficient of x to the n minus 100.

55
00:08:41,708 --> 00:08:51,351
The negative binomial series, with x squared in place of x, gives i plus 99 choose 99, times x to the 2 i.

56
00:08:51,801 --> 00:09:01,123
The coefficient row starts at x to the 100. Every odd position is 0, because 100 odd numbers have an even sum.

57
00:09:01,573 --> 00:09:16,315
The even positions are i plus 99 choose 99, for i equal to 0, 1, 2 and 3. That is 1, 100, 5050 and 171700.

58
00:09:16,765 --> 00:09:27,325
We need 2 i equal to n minus 100. So i is n over 2 minus 50, and i plus 99 is n over 2 plus 49.

59
00:09:27,775 --> 00:09:36,713
The answer is n over 2 plus 49, choose 99, when n is even and at least 100. Otherwise it is 0.

60
00:09:37,163 --> 00:09:51,329
Assignment 3, question 2 b. Part i must have the same parity as i: part 1 odd, part 2 even, part 3 odd, and so on. Prove the formula for the count. Pause the video and try it.

61
00:09:51,779 --> 00:09:55,363
Here are the five compositions of 7 with this property.

62
00:09:55,813 --> 00:10:02,106
Odd parts are blue, and even parts are purple. The first part is odd, and the colours alternate.

63
00:10:02,556 --> 00:10:12,049
Group the parts in pairs from the left: an odd part, then an even part. 1 2 1 2 1 is two pairs and one odd part at the end.

64
00:10:12,499 --> 00:10:20,841
If the last part is odd, the composition is k pairs and then one odd part. These compositions form S 1.

65
00:10:21,291 --> 00:10:30,955
If the last part is even, or there are no parts, the composition is k pairs only. These form S 2. S 1 and S 2 are disjoint.

66
00:10:31,405 --> 00:10:39,853
The odd parts have the series x over 1 minus x squared. The even parts have x squared over 1 minus x squared.

67
00:10:40,303 --> 00:10:47,129
By the Product and String Lemmas, S 2 has the series 1 over 1 minus phi O times phi E.

68
00:10:47,579 --> 00:10:51,782
By the Product Lemma, S 1 has the same series, times phi O.

69
00:10:52,232 --> 00:10:58,355
By the Sum Lemma, add them: 1 plus phi O, over 1 minus phi O times phi E.

70
00:10:58,805 --> 00:11:10,303
Multiply the top and the bottom by 1 minus x squared, squared. The top becomes 1 plus x minus 2 x squared minus x cubed plus x to the 4.

71
00:11:10,753 --> 00:11:18,753
The bottom becomes 1 minus 2 x squared minus x cubed plus x to the 4. This is the fraction in the question.

72
00:11:19,203 --> 00:11:36,270
Check n equal to 7. From the bottom, s n is 2 s n minus 2, plus s n minus 3, minus s n minus 4. At x to the 7: 2 times 3, plus 1, minus 2, is 5. This matches the five compositions.

73
00:11:36,720 --> 00:11:40,667
Assignment 3, question 3. Pause the video and try it.

74
00:11:41,117 --> 00:11:51,805
For a n, the parts are 1, 4, 7, and so on. Their series is x plus x to the 4, and so on, which is x over 1 minus x cubed.

75
00:11:52,255 --> 00:12:09,236
Any length is allowed. By the String Lemma, A is 1 over 1 minus x over 1 minus x cubed. Multiply the top and the bottom by 1 minus x cubed. A is 1 minus x cubed, over 1 minus x minus x cubed.

76
00:12:09,686 --> 00:12:20,907
For b n, the parts are 3, 4, 5, and so on. Their series is x cubed plus x to the 4, and so on, which is x cubed over 1 minus x.

77
00:12:21,357 --> 00:12:29,997
By the String Lemma, B is 1 minus x, over 1 minus x minus x cubed. A and B have the same denominator.

78
00:12:30,447 --> 00:12:42,394
To compare a n with b n plus 2, multiply A by x squared. This moves its coefficients two places to the right. Here are the rows of x squared A and of B.

79
00:12:42,844 --> 00:12:47,260
The two rows agree from x cubed on. The green cells show this.

80
00:12:47,710 --> 00:12:55,027
Their difference is minus 1 at x to the 0, then 0, then 1 at x squared, and 0 after that.

81
00:12:55,477 --> 00:13:08,747
Prove it with algebra. The numerator of x squared A minus B is x squared minus x to the 5, minus 1, plus x. This is x squared minus 1, times the denominator.

82
00:13:09,197 --> 00:13:24,834
So x squared A minus B is x squared minus 1. It has no terms of degree 3 or more. So a k minus 2 equals b k for k at least 3. That is, a n equals b n plus 2 for n at least 1.

83
00:13:25,284 --> 00:13:29,252
Practice midterm, question 3. Pause the video and try it.

84
00:13:29,702 --> 00:13:42,481
Step 1. The allowed parts are the numbers congruent to 2 or 3 mod 5: 2, 3, 7, 8, 12, 13, and so on. They are the blue cells of the first row.

85
00:13:42,931 --> 00:13:55,283
Take out x squared plus x cubed. The rest is a geometric series in x to the 5. So phi of P is x squared plus x cubed, over 1 minus x to the 5.

86
00:13:55,733 --> 00:14:00,853
Step 2. The allowed lengths are 1, 6, 11, and so on. They are purple.

87
00:14:01,303 --> 00:14:08,876
So the set is the disjoint union over j of P to the 5 j plus 1. The weight is the size.

88
00:14:09,326 --> 00:14:25,966
By the Sum and Product Lemmas, phi of S is the sum of phi of P to the 5 j plus 1. Take out one factor. The rest is a geometric series in phi of P to the 5. Phi of P has no constant term, so this is valid.

89
00:14:26,416 --> 00:14:38,683
The green row shows the coefficients. Six parts have size at least 12. So for n up to 11, only one part fits, and the count is 1 at 2, 3, 7 and 8.

90
00:14:39,133 --> 00:14:51,570
At n equal to 12 the count is 2: the composition 12, and six parts equal to 2. A brute force count agrees with every coefficient up to n equal to 25.

91
00:14:52,020 --> 00:15:04,671
The question forbids an infinite sum. So multiply the top and the bottom by 1 minus x to the 5, to the 5. The answer is the coefficient of x to the n in this fraction.

92
00:15:05,121 --> 00:15:11,521
Recap. Any length: the set is P star, and the series is 1 over 1 minus phi of P.

93
00:15:11,971 --> 00:15:17,133
Exactly k parts: P to the k, and the series is phi of P to the k.

94
00:15:17,583 --> 00:15:29,231
Length congruent to r mod m: the union of P to the m j plus r. The series is phi of P to the r, over 1 minus phi of P to the m.

95
00:15:29,681 --> 00:15:40,881
At least k parts: cut after part k. The set is P to the k times P star, and the series is phi of P to the k over 1 minus phi of P.

96
00:15:41,331 --> 00:15:46,750
Parts whose type alternates: group them in pairs, and add an optional last part.

97
00:15:47,200 --> 00:15:54,837
In every answer, define the set and the weight, name each lemma, and check a small n by listing the compositions.
